{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-86429" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=6{x}^{2}+2y$ at the point $(0,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=3i+5j.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-86429" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{5}^{2}}=\\sqrt{34} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}}(3i+5j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6{x}^{2}+2y)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6{x}^{2}+2y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =12x\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇\\,f(0,1) & =(12\\cdot 0)\\,i+2\\,j \\\\  & =2j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(0,1) & =∇\\,f(0,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =2j\\cdot (\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{34}}i+\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{34}}j) \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 5}{\\sqrt{34}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\sqrt{34}}{34}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-86429" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c362cd907fb85830ab3320317ae756b56808309a5fb6a6d98274061b8fc74a3f"} {"question" "q-86429" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-86429" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{10\\sqrt{34}}{34}"] ["math" "\\frac{10\\sqrt{43}}{34}"] ["math" "\\frac{11\\sqrt{34}}{34}"] ["math" "\\frac{11\\sqrt{43}}{43}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{34}}{34}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-86429" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{10\\sqrt{34}}{34}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-36290" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}y$ at the point $A(1,0)$ in the direction towards the point $B(0,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-36290" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨0,1⟩-⟨1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}}⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}y),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{y}+2xy,\\,x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,0) & =⟨{e}^{0}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot 0,\\,1\\cdot {e}^{0}+{1}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(1,0) & =∇f(1,0)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-36290" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c362cd907fb85830ab3320317ae756b56808309a5fb6a6d98274061b8fc74a3f"} {"question" "q-36290" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-36290" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-36290" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-130260" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ at the point $(0,-1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=3\\,i+4\\,j.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-130260" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}=5 \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\,i+4\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(0,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}))\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}j \\\\ ∇\\,f(0,-1) & =\\frac{2(0)}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2(-1)}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(0,-1) & =∇\\,f(0,-1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =-2\\,j\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}(3\\,i+4\\,j) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2(4)}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-130260" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c362cd907fb85830ab3320317ae756b56808309a5fb6a6d98274061b8fc74a3f"} {"question" "q-130260" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-130260" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3"] ["math" "-\\frac{8}{5}"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-130260" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{8}{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-130257" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=xy$ at the point $(2,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=\\sqrt{3}\\,i+j.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-130257" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{1}^{2}}=2 \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\,i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =y\\,i+x\\,j \\\\ ∇\\,f(2,1) & =i+2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(2,1) & =∇\\,f(2,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =(i+2\\,j)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1(\\sqrt{3})+2(1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+2}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-130257" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c362cd907fb85830ab3320317ae756b56808309a5fb6a6d98274061b8fc74a3f"} {"question" "q-130257" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-130257" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+2}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+4}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{3}-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-130257" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+2}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-130263" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=i-j.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-130263" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i-j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy)j \\\\  & =(2x-y)\\,i+(2y-x)\\,j \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,1) & =(2(1)-1)\\,i+(2(1)-1)\\,j \\\\  & =i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(1,1) & =∇\\,f(1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =(i+j)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i-j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1(1)+1(-1)}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-130263" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c362cd907fb85830ab3320317ae756b56808309a5fb6a6d98274061b8fc74a3f"} {"question" "q-130263" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-130263" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-130263" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-37080" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x+y)+3{z}^{2}x$, in what direction does the function increase most rapidly at the point $(1,0,1)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-37080" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "At a given point, a function $f(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x+y)+3{z}^{2}x),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x+y)+3{z}^{2}x),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x+y)+3{z}^{2}x)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x+y)+3{z}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x+y),\\,6xz⟩ \\\\ ∇f(1,0,1) & =⟨\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1+0)+3\\cdot {1}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1+0),\\,6\\cdot 1\\cdot 1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+3,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ),\\,6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\pi +3,\\,-1,\\,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨-\\pi +3,\\,-1,\\,6⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-37080" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c362cd907fb85830ab3320317ae756b56808309a5fb6a6d98274061b8fc74a3f"} {"question" "q-37080" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-37080" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨-\\pi +3,\\,-1,\\,6⟩"] ["math" "⟨-\\pi +3,\\,\\pi ,\\,3⟩"] ["math" "⟨2,\\,-1,\\,6⟩"] ["math" "⟨3,\\,-\\pi ,\\,3⟩"] ["math" "⟨3,\\,0,\\,6⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-37080" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨-\\pi +3,\\,-1,\\,6⟩"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-130276" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+3xy+2{y}^{2}$ increase most rapidly at the point $(2,1)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-130276" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+3xy+2{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+3xy+2{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(2x+3y)\\,i+(3x+4y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,1) & =(2(2)+3(1))\\,i+(3(2)+4(1))\\,j \\\\  & =7\\,i+10\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $7\\,i+10\\,j.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-130276" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c362cd907fb85830ab3320317ae756b56808309a5fb6a6d98274061b8fc74a3f"} {"question" "q-130276" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-130276" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\,i+j"] ["math" "3\\,i-5\\,j"] ["math" "4\\,i+j"] ["math" "7\\,i+10\\,j"] ["math" "i-2\\,j"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-130276" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "7\\,i+10\\,j"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-83554" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y{e}^{x}-xy$ increase most rapidly at the point $(-1,1)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-83554" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y{e}^{x}-xy)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y{e}^{x}-xy)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y{e}^{x}-y)\\text{ }i+(\\frac{x}{y}+{e}^{x}-x)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(1,1) & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+1\\cdot {e}^{-1}-1)\\text{ }i+(\\frac{-1}{1}+{e}^{-1}-(-1))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =({e}^{-1}-1)\\text{ }i+{e}^{-1}j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $({e}^{-1}-1)\\text{ }i+{e}^{-1}j.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-83554" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c362cd907fb85830ab3320317ae756b56808309a5fb6a6d98274061b8fc74a3f"} {"question" "q-83554" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-83554" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(e+1)\\text{ }i+(e-1)j"] ["math" "(e-1)\\text{ }i+ej"] ["math" "({e}^{-1}+1)\\text{ }i+({e}^{-1}-1)j"] ["math" "({e}^{-1}+1)\\text{ }i+{e}^{-1}j"] ["math" "({e}^{-1}-1)\\text{ }i+{e}^{-1}j"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-83554" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "({e}^{-1}-1)\\text{ }i+{e}^{-1}j"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-36305" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)={y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x+\\pi z)$ increase most rapidly at the point $(1,1,2)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-36305" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x+\\pi z)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x+\\pi z)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x+\\pi z))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x+\\pi z),\\,2y,\\,\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x+\\pi z)⟩ \\\\ ∇g(1,1,2) & =⟨2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi \\cdot 1+\\pi \\cdot 2),\\,2\\cdot 1,\\,\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi \\cdot 1+\\pi \\cdot 2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\pi ),\\,2,\\,\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\pi )⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\pi ,\\,2,\\,\\pi ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨2\\pi ,\\,2,\\,\\pi ⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-36305" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c362cd907fb85830ab3320317ae756b56808309a5fb6a6d98274061b8fc74a3f"} {"question" "q-36305" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-36305" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨-2\\pi ,-2,-\\pi ⟩"] ["math" "⟨-\\pi ,-1,\\pi ⟩"] ["math" "⟨2,2,1⟩"] ["math" "⟨2\\pi ,2,\\pi ⟩"] ["math" "⟨\\pi ,2,\\pi ⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-36305" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨2\\pi ,2,\\pi ⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-86537" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)=3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ increase most rapidly at the point $(0,1)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-86537" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+6x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\text{ }i+(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(0,1) & =(1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+6\\cdot 0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))\\text{ }i+(3(0)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $-j.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-86537" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c362cd907fb85830ab3320317ae756b56808309a5fb6a6d98274061b8fc74a3f"} {"question" "q-86537" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-86537" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-j"] ["math" "2i-j"] ["math" "i+j"] ["math" "i-j"] ["math" "j"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-86537" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-j"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-37083" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x,y)=(x+y)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y).$ What is the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(2,-1)$ in the direction in which the function decreases most rapidly?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-37083" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the opposite direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $-∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇\\,f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x+y)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x+y)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+1)\\text{ }i+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+1)\\text{ }j \\\\ -∇\\,f(2,-1) & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-1)+1)i-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-1)+1)j \\\\  & =-i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(2,-1)$ in the direction of $-∇f(2,-1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-∇\\,f(2,-1)‖ & =‖-i-j‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-37083" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c362cd907fb85830ab3320317ae756b56808309a5fb6a6d98274061b8fc74a3f"} {"question" "q-37083" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-37083" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-37083" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-130801" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $g(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{y}{x}.$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(-1,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=⟨1,-\\sqrt{2}⟩?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-130801" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(-1,1)$ in the direction of $v$ is equal to the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(-1,1).$ \n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-\\sqrt{2})^{2}}=\\sqrt{3} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1,-\\sqrt{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{y}{x}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{y}{x})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{y}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}⟩ \\\\ ∇g(-1,1) & =⟨\\frac{1}{1}-\\frac{1}{(-1)^{2}},\\frac{1}{(-1)}-\\frac{(-1)}{{1}^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(-1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(-1,1) & =∇g(-1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨0,0⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1,-\\sqrt{2}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{0(1)+0(-\\sqrt{2})}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-130801" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c362cd907fb85830ab3320317ae756b56808309a5fb6a6d98274061b8fc74a3f"} {"question" "q-130801" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-130801" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-130801" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-83613" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $g(x,y)=2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡y-2y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ in the direction of the vector $v=⟨-1,1⟩?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-83613" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ in the direction of $v$ is equal to the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ \n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient $∇\\,g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡y-2y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡y-2y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{tan}⁡y-2y{sec}^{2}⁡x,\\,2x{sec}^{2}⁡y-2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,g(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\cdot 1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 2,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2-\\pi ,\\,\\pi -2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =∇\\,g(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨2-\\pi ,\\pi -2⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{(2-\\pi )\\cdot (-1)+(\\pi -2)\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi -4}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(\\pi -2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-83613" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c362cd907fb85830ab3320317ae756b56808309a5fb6a6d98274061b8fc74a3f"} {"question" "q-83613" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-83613" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2(\\pi +2)"] ["math" "2(\\pi -2)"] ["math" "\\pi -\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}(\\pi +2)"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}(\\pi -2)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-83613" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{2}(\\pi -2)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-83608" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $g(x,y)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)}{y}+\\pi xy.$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=⟨-2,2⟩?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-83608" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of $v$ is equal to the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(1,1).$ \n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}}=2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient $∇\\,g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)}{y}+\\pi xy),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)}{y}+\\pi xy)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)}{y}+\\pi y,\\,-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)}{{y}^{2}}+\\pi x⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,g(1,1) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)}{1}+\\pi \\cdot 1,\\,-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)}{{1}^{2}}+\\pi \\cdot 1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\pi +\\pi ,0+\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\pi ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(1,1) & =∇\\,g(1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨0,\\pi ⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot (-1)+\\pi \\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-83608" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c362cd907fb85830ab3320317ae756b56808309a5fb6a6d98274061b8fc74a3f"} {"question" "q-83608" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-83608" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2\\pi "] ["math" "-\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}"] ["math" "\\pi \\sqrt{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-83608" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-36300" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x,y)=2xy+{e}^{3x-y}.$ What is the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,3)$ in the direction in which the function decreases most rapidly?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-36300" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the opposite direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $-∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇\\,f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy+{e}^{3x-y})\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy+{e}^{3x-y})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-(2y+3{e}^{3x-y})\\text{ }i-(2x-{e}^{3x-y})\\text{ }j \\\\ -∇\\,f(1,3) & =-(2\\cdot 3+3\\cdot {e}^{3\\cdot 1-3})\\text{ }i-(2\\cdot 1-{e}^{3\\cdot 1-3})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-9\\text{ }i-\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,3)$ in the direction of $-∇f(1,3)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-∇\\,f(1,3)‖ & =‖-9\\text{ }i-\\text{ }j‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-9)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{82}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-36300" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c362cd907fb85830ab3320317ae756b56808309a5fb6a6d98274061b8fc74a3f"} {"question" "q-36300" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-36300" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "9"] ["math" "\\sqrt{10}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{80}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{82}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{8}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "q-36300" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{82}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "4376702b89825ead4f855d51ff612fc2873c7f6e8d7c2ccea7cc869d52433740"} {"question" "e-3377" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the directional derivative of the function $g(x, y) =3x^2+4xy$ at the point $A(0,1)$ in the direction towards the point $B(1,4).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/example-3377.json" "file_sha256" "4b5d0ce2f6dac2876855391d7dad8b4e7ca965fdf5d6157c9b9717f539e87849"} {"question" "e-3377" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The directional derivative $g'_{\\mathbf u}(a,b)$ of a function $g(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf v$ is given by\n\n$$\ng'_{\\mathbf u}(a,b) = \\nabla g(a,b)\\cdot\\mathbf u\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $\\mathbf v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨1,4⟩-⟨0,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,3⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}}⟨1,3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $\\nabla g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(0,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}+4xy),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}+4xy)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6x+4y,\\,4x⟩ \\\\ ∇g(0,1) & =⟨6\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 1,\\,4\\cdot 0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(0,1) & =∇g(0,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨4,0⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}},\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/example-3377.json" "file_sha256" "4b5d0ce2f6dac2876855391d7dad8b4e7ca965fdf5d6157c9b9717f539e87849"} {"question" "e-3381" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "In what direction does the function $f (x, y) = \\ln (x^2y)$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(2, 1)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/example-3381.json" "file_sha256" "fb6b6c86b31a4ce60405fda2214b9c9207b598810976e988aaa6517afd62ed36"} {"question" "e-3381" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $-\\nabla f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}y))\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}y))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}\\cdot 2xy\\text{ }i-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}\\cdot {x}^{2}j \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{x}\\text{ }i-\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j \\\\ -∇f(2,1) & =-\\frac{2}{2}\\text{ }i-\\frac{1}{1}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the direction of $- \\, \\mathbf i - \\mathbf j.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/example-3381.json" "file_sha256" "fb6b6c86b31a4ce60405fda2214b9c9207b598810976e988aaa6517afd62ed36"} {"question" "e-3382" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $g (x, y) = \\ln \\left(\\sqrt {xy^3}\\right).$ What is the rate of change of $g$ at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)$ in the direction in which the function increases most rapidly?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/example-3382.json" "file_sha256" "4e464914cb6ae71045d3ab7a37b627f7bc84630eb6db147fbc54bd5c659a3259"} {"question" "e-3382" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "At a given point, a function $g(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $\\nabla g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)\\!\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{2\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}}\\cdot \\frac{3x{y}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2x},\\,\\frac{3}{2y}⟩ \\\\ ∇g(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}) & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})},\\frac{3}{2\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2})}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)$ in the direction of $\\nabla g\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖∇g(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2})‖ & =‖⟨1,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/example-3382.json" "file_sha256" "4e464914cb6ae71045d3ab7a37b627f7bc84630eb6db147fbc54bd5c659a3259"} {"question" "q-36305" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨2\\pi ,2,\\pi ⟩"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "2d2a06101f390ea110937dc0dc6a084431f1df1d0b17f598f4afd78d454d2243" "reviewer_session" "01a11908-fa8d-7c61-8e03-c1185d0b9506" "rationale" "The gradient is ⟨2π cos(2πx+πz),2y,π cos(2πx+πz)⟩. At (1,1,2), the cosine is cos(4π)=1, giving ⟨2π,2,π⟩. A differentiable function increases most rapidly in the direction of its nonzero gradient, so original option c is correct. The authentic solution, stored key, and saved Correct grading agree." "bindings" {"problem" "323355ad638ce46b9241dbcece5b767e162bd5012d964bb838a38297c4c8ab5d" "worked_solution" "3a9087080106efa6bb6f2845427d2ebbf388a5bd5ae187f2a5b8e4e2c3b54e3c" "original_field" "9f44f93b0454b2352b7eb8ab6e41d5e585104bc52dadb165b2b490abcc27e14d" "reviewed_field" "58e0a0f3e8d7dfb5db62d1edacad9fd478c9b8e428c19861487bae5b0675aa8f" "raw_widget" "789cac3711fd2db5af2b84e81694bf781ed0ea32d480e9f782dca0a19feb5ef3"} "evidence_files" [{"path" "q-36305-before.png" "sha256" "5ac7dc533741f68adfe895894c9acbbc27ced4e83495224814271a1e8fbc10bf"} {"path" "q-36305-before.json" "sha256" "1230256c9f8e19846d52ad647149f409fa5788f487db6e0298a6e72011a6225c"} {"path" "history-q-36305.png" "sha256" "e5fe49be0988821cf26bdbe8cbf0702ceb144f844632f4a41542046bb8f8805e"} {"path" "history-q-36305.json" "sha256" "53db61ee5d3723b190795550ffdf655bc40191d4720732cd68776775aa9a3ebc"} {"path" "q-36305-after.json" "sha256" "3475e6a26eb12a34b47f8613fdd5c46af9745d93398b8c303380036cbed644e0"} {"path" "edb-import/questions.edn" "sha256" "6efcc234c22404c9a5907bf94cf6cee7347231b8308104e1f9dcb40ed7ada986"}]} {"question" "q-36305" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨-2\\pi ,-2,-\\pi ⟩"] ["math" "⟨-\\pi ,-1,\\pi ⟩"] ["math" "⟨2,2,1⟩"] ["math" "⟨2\\pi ,2,\\pi ⟩"] ["math" "⟨\\pi ,2,\\pi ⟩"]] "category" "reviewed_ma_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "2d2a06101f390ea110937dc0dc6a084431f1df1d0b17f598f4afd78d454d2243" "rationale" "The gradient is ⟨2π cos(2πx+πz),2y,π cos(2πx+πz)⟩. At (1,1,2), the cosine is cos(4π)=1, giving ⟨2π,2,π⟩. A differentiable function increases most rapidly in the direction of its nonzero gradient, so original option c is correct. The authentic solution, stored key, and saved Correct grading agree."}] "usage" {"e-3382" [] "e-3381" [] "q-36305" [] "e-3377" []} "basis" 2393 "retractions" [[17592186124881 :question/problem "In what direction does the function $g(x, y, z) = y^{2} + \\sin (2\\pi x + \\pi z)$ increase most rapidly at the point $(1, 1, 2)$?"] [17592186124881 :question/worked-solution "The gradient is $$\\nabla g=\\langle2\\pi\\cos(2\\pi x+\\pi z),2y,\\pi\\cos(2\\pi x+\\pi z)\\rangle.$$ At $(1,1,2)$ the cosine is $\\cos4\\pi=1$, so $\\nabla g=\\langle2\\pi,2,\\pi\\rangle$. This nonzero gradient points in the direction of greatest increase."] [17592186124881 :question/answer-fields 17592186122308] [17592186124177 :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $g(x, y) =3x^2+4xy$ at the point $A(0,1)$ in the direction towards the point $B(1,4)$."] [17592186124177 :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative $g'_{\\mathbf u}(a,b)$ of a function $g(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf v$ is given by\n\n$$\ng'_{\\mathbf u}(a,b) = \\nabla g(a,b)\\cdot\\mathbf u\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $\\mathbf v$.\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\overrightarrow{AB} & = \\langle  1 , 4 \\rangle  - \\langle  0 , 1 \\rangle \\\\  & = \\langle  1 , 3 \\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf{u} & = \\frac{\\overrightarrow{AB}}{\\Vert  \\overrightarrow{AB} \\Vert} \\\\  & = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2} + {3}^{2}}} \\langle  1 , 3 \\rangle \\\\  & = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\langle  1 , 3 \\rangle\\end{array}$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $\\nabla g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(0,1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla g(x, y) &= \\langle \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial x}(3x^{2} + 4xy), \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial y}(3x^{2} + 4xy)\\rangle  \\\\\n&= \\langle 6x + 4y, 4x\\rangle  \\\\\n\\nabla g(0, 1) &= \\langle 6 \\cdot 0 + 4 \\cdot 1, 4 \\cdot 0\\rangle  \\\\\n&= \\langle 4, 0\\rangle \n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_{u}^{\\prime }(0, 1) &= \\nabla g(0, 1) \\cdot u \\\\\n&= \\langle 4, 0\\rangle  \\cdot \\langle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}, \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\rangle  \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186124178 :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f (x, y) = \\ln (x^2y)$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(2, 1)$?"] [17592186124178 :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $-\\nabla f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n- \\nabla f(x, y) &= -\\frac{\\partial }{\\partial x}(\\ln (x^{2}y))i - \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial y}(\\ln (x^{2}y))j \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{x^{2}y} \\cdot 2xyi - \\frac{1}{x^{2}y} \\cdot x^{2}j \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{x}i - \\frac{1}{y}j \\\\\n- \\nabla f(2, 1) &= -\\frac{2}{2}i - \\frac{1}{1}j \\\\\n&=-i - j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the direction of $-\\, \\mathbf i - \\mathbf j$."] [17592186124179 :question/problem "Consider the function $g (x, y) = \\ln \\left(\\sqrt {xy^3}\\right)$. What is the rate of change of $g$ at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)$ in the direction in which the function increases most rapidly?"] [17592186124179 :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $\\nabla g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)\\!\n$$\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\mathrm{\\nabla } g ( x , y ) & = \\langle  \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  x} ( \\ln   ( \\sqrt{x {y}^{3}} ) ) , \\, \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  y} ( \\ln   ( \\sqrt{x {y}^{3}} ) ) \\rangle \\\\  & = \\langle  \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x {y}^{3}}} \\cdot  \\frac{{y}^{3}}{2 \\sqrt{x {y}^{3}}} , \\, \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x {y}^{3}}} \\cdot  \\frac{3 x {y}^{2}}{2 \\sqrt{x {y}^{3}}} \\rangle \\\\  & = \\langle  \\frac{1}{2 x} , \\, \\frac{3}{2 y} \\rangle \\\\ \\mathrm{\\nabla } g ( \\frac{1}{2} , \\frac{3}{2} ) & = \\langle  \\frac{1}{2 \\cdot  ( \\frac{1}{2} )} , \\frac{3}{2 \\cdot  ( \\frac{3}{2} )} \\rangle \\\\  & = \\langle  1 , 1 \\rangle\\end{array}$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)\n$$\n\nin the direction of\n\n$$\n\\nabla g\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Vert \\nabla g(\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{3}{2})\\Vert  &= \\Vert \\langle 1, 1\\rangle \\Vert  \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1^{2} + 1^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186122309 17592186122310 17592186122311 17592186122312 17592186122313] "immutable_fields" [[17592186122308 :answer-field/correct] [17592186122308 :answer-field/id] [17592186122308 :answer-field/key] [17592186122308 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
